        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4c494d5e-c2b2-50f1-9e02-0fd708c5d7e9",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Blechnum, Hakea, Banksia blechnifolia\n- Places: Southern Hemisphere, Northern Hemisphere, Cape Horn, Chile, Iceland, northern Norway, Australia\n- Concepts: species diversity, plant naming\n\nFacts:\n- Blechnum has its greatest species diversity in tropical regions of the Southern Hemisphere.\n- Only a few Blechnum species reach cool temperate latitudes in the Southern Hemisphere and Northern Hemisphere.\n- Blechnum penna-marina occurs south to Cape Horn, Chile, and it is the southernmost fern in the world.\n- Blechnum spicant occurs north to Iceland and northern Norway.\n- Hakea, also called pincushion tree, is a genus of 149 species of shrubs and small trees in the Proteaceae and is native to Australia.\n- Banksia blechnifolia gained its specific name because its leaves resemble the leaves of the fern genus Blechnum.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "c2ba53a9-a23d-5957-8a44-cbec0510fa94",
        "text": "Page: Blechnum. Sentence: By far the greatest species diversity is in tropical regions of the Southern Hemisphere, with only a few species reaching cool temperate latitudes in the Southern Hemisphere (notably \"B. penna-marina\", south to Cape Horn, Chile, the southernmost fern in the world) and Northern Hemisphere (notably \"B. spicant\", north to Iceland and northern Norway).\nPage: Hakea. Sentence: Hakea (pincushion tree) is a genus of 149 species of shrubs and small trees in the Proteaceae, native to Australia.\nPage: Banksia blechnifolia. Sentence: It gained its specific name as its leaves are reminiscent of fern (\"Blechnum\") leaves.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
